<title>ARGENTINA: Chile's Escondida copper workers start strike vote.</title>
<headline>Chile's Escondida copper workers start strike vote.</headline>
<dateline>BUENOS AIRES 1997-04-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Striking workers at Chile's Escondida copper mine began voting at 1219 local (1619 GMT) Wednesday on whether to accept the firm's latest pay offer, a union official said.</p>
<p>The mine's 350-odd unionized workers stopped work Friday to demand progress in wage negotiations. The rest of the mine's 2,000-strong workforce did not join the strike.</p>
<p>Mine CEO Ken Pickering said Monday that the strike had slown down operations slightly but that output of both concentrates and cathodes was normal.</p>
<p>The 800,000-tonne-a-year mine is owned by Broken Hill Proprietary and RTZ Corp Plc</p>
<p>((Jason Webb, Buenos Aires Newsroom +541 318-0655))</p>
